# What does NOT go into a public repo, and why.
#
# kaolin/crew is PUBLIC on GitHub. The hub's own tooling is fine to publish --
# it is generic fleet plumbing. What is not fine is the residue that tooling
# leaves behind, because that residue is made of his private work. So the rule
# is: COMMIT THE MACHINE, IGNORE WHAT WENT THROUGH IT.
#
# Each entry below is a class, not a file, so new residue of the same kind is
# excluded automatically instead of relying on someone noticing it.

# Approver and watcher logs. These are verbatim SCREEN SCRAPES of worker
# sessions -- command lines, file paths, diffs, prompt text -- from private
# repos. The single most sensitive thing in this directory.
*.log

# One-off relay drafts. Each is a message written for one session about one
# piece of in-flight work: unreleased features, pricing decisions, store
# strategy. No reuse value, and publishing them would publish the decisions.
msg-*.txt

# One-off fan-out runners. They hardcode session names and point at the
# msg-*.txt files above, so they neither work elsewhere nor mean anything
# without the drafts. `fanout.sh` (no hyphen) IS generic and IS committed.
fanout-*.sh

# Live operational state, rewritten constantly. Committing it would mean a diff
# every time a session starts or stops, and it is a roster of what he is
# working on rather than a tool.
approver-covered.txt

# App-specific one-offs: a worker brief carrying one app's unshipped bug
# details, and an assembler for a private docs tree. Both reference work that
# is his to disclose, not the hub's.
timeful-task.md
assemble-cheat.py

# Hand-rolled backups (*.bak-YYYYMMDD, *.prev-YYYYMMDD). These predate the
# scripts being tracked at all -- they ARE the thing git is for. Once history
# exists they are noise, and in a public repo they are confusing noise.
*.bak-*
*.prev-*
